  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical analysis is restricted to the Japanese port system and its specific relationship with the Busan hub, which may limit generalizability to other national port-hinterland configurations.",
      "what": "This study analyzes how global container shipping networks shape domestic port hinterlands in Japan by combining a longitudinal analysis of inland distance friction from 1985 to 2018 with cross-sectional spatial interaction models for 2018 using CCFS and AIS data. It finds that feeder connectivity to the foreign transhipment hub of Busan, South Korea, is associated with strong but spatially bounded hinterland capture, particularly for exports, whereas trunk-line connectivity shows weak and inconsistent effects.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that global maritime network integration reshapes rather than eliminates spatial frictions, showing that regional feeder networks to foreign hubs can be more influential for domestic cargo capture than direct trunk-line connections."
    }
